# File Share & NTFS Permissions Lab

## Objective
Demonstrate structured file share creation, security group-based access control, NTFS vs Share permission understanding, and access denied troubleshooting in an Active Directory environment.

Lab Environment:
- Domain: corp.local
- Domain Controller: DC1 (Windows Server 2022)
- Client Workstation: PC1 (Windows 11 Pro)
- User: J.Smith

---

# Architecture Overview

Security Model Used:

Users → Security Groups → Folder Permissions

Direct user-to-folder permissions were intentionally avoided to follow best practices.

Security Group Created:
GG_Sales_RW (Global Security Group)

Shared Folder:
\\DC1\Sales

Local Path:
C:\Shares\Sales

---

# Phase 1 – Security Group Configuration

1. Created global security group:
   GG_Sales_RW
2. Added user:
   J.Smith
3. Verified group membership under user properties.

---

# Phase 2 – Share Configuration

Configured Advanced Sharing on:

C:\Shares\Sales

Share Name:
Sales

Share Permissions:
- Domain Admins → Full Control
- GG_Sales_RW → Change
- Removed Everyone

---

# Phase 3 – NTFS Permissions

Configured Security tab on:

C:\Shares\Sales

NTFS Permissions:
- Domain Admins → Full Control
- GG_Sales_RW → Modify
- SYSTEM retained

Verified inheritance and effective permission structure.

---

# Validation (Initial Access Test)

On PC1:

Accessed:
\\DC01\Sales

User successfully:
- Opened folder
- Created test file
- Deleted test file

Confirmed correct access behavior.

---

# Failure Simulation – Access Denied Scenario

To simulate real-world troubleshooting:

1. Removed J.Smith from GG_Sales_RW.
2. Logged out/in to refresh security token.
3. Attempted access to:
   \\DC1\Sales

Result:
Access Denied.

---

# Troubleshooting Process

1. Verified network connectivity:
   ping DC01

2. Confirmed UNC path correct.

3. Checked user group membership in ADUC.
   Observed missing membership in GG_Sales_RW.

Root Cause:
User not assigned to required security group for share access.

---

# Resolution

1. Re-added J.Smith to GG_Sales_RW.
2. Logged user out and back in.
3. Retested share access.

Access restored successfully.

---

